Pseudo2Wasm development and verification log
Date: 2026-09-25 (America/New_York)
Verified revision: b06e8af2f2550ffe6ea520ba37f9ced82c5972a0
Commit: Improve compiler architecture, diagnostics, and benchmarks
Package version: 1.2.0 (no release/publish performed)
Node: v22.23.1

Changes recorded
- Default Wasm removes unreachable functions and imports.
- Callable frames use one capacity check while retaining overflow diagnostics.
- execute() releases temporary Binaryen modules and decodes only string bytes.
- CompilationError.message includes all collected diagnostics and source carets.
  Compilation collects up to 20 errors in the earliest failing phase; runtime
  execution stops at its first failure.
- Added portable Emscripten/AssemblyScript comparison workloads, result checks,
  warmed/rotated timing batches, raw reports, and performance documentation.
- Started Milestone 7: AssemblyScript-inspired architecture and value lowering.
  Added read-only syntax traversal, parameter analysis, and FunctionContext;
  unified callable emission and return cleanup.
- Immutable basic BYVAL parameter reads can use Wasm parameters directly.
  Pointer/BYREF programs conservatively opt out. Frame layout, initialization
  stores, and checks remain. General mutable-local promotion and frameless
  calls are not implemented.
- Previous roadmap milestones 7 and 8 are now 8 and 9.

Fresh verification
Command: npm test
- Type checking and browser/Node/declaration/test builds completed.
- 25 existing Node tests passed.
- 20 benchmark/codegen tests passed.
- 48 default/O2 differential cases passed.
- 12 Wasm-size and host-call budgets passed.
- Headless Edge tests and packed npm Node/browser entry checks passed.
- Build retains the known 14.4 MiB browser compiler bundle warnings.

Performance evidence (previously measured; not rerun for this log update)
See BENCHMARKS.md and benchmarks/comparison/results-*.json.
The parameter-read follow-up reduced O2 matrix Wasm from 989 to 947 bytes and
Fibonacci from 313 to 296 bytes. Follow-up timings came from one full run;
they are preliminary, not a guaranteed speedup or a universal compiler ranking.

